# Copy to .env.local. Never commit .env.local — these are database credentials.
#
# Codroon POS gets a Supabase project OF ITS OWN, in whatever organisation you
# like. Not the project the codroon.com website uses, for four reasons:
#
#   · the website holds YOUR leads (Codroon is the controller); this holds your
#     shops' customers — names, phones, DNI numbers — where Codroon is only the
#     processor (ADR-0020 §4). "Delete this shop" must not be a query that can
#     reach your marketing data.
#   · one project has one auth.users table, so sharing it would put estimate-form
#     signups and shop owners in the same identity pool.
#   · a public marketing site with forms is the most-probed thing you own.
#   · region. This project must be EU (Frankfurt, eu-central-1) and a Supabase
#     project's region cannot be changed after it is created.
#
# Project settings → Database → Connection string. TWO strings, not
# interchangeable.

# --- Transaction pooler, port 6543. What the running app uses: a request checks
# a connection out, runs, gives it back. `prepare: false` in src/db/client.ts
# goes with this — prepared statements cannot survive a pooled connection being
# reused by somebody else.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-eu-central-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres"

# --- Session pooler, port 5432. What `pnpm db:migrate` uses. DDL through a
# TRANSACTION pooler is a way to spend an afternoon, so migrations need a
# connection they keep for the whole session.
#
# Note the host: this is the pooler on port 5432, NOT the "Direct connection"
# string the dashboard also offers (db.PROJECT.supabase.co:5432). That one is
# IPv6-only on new projects, so it fails from most Windows machines and most CI.
# The session pooler is the IPv4 answer and does the same job here.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-eu-central-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# --- Auth (ADR-0021 §1). Not read by anything yet — the sign-in slice is next.
# Copy them while you are in the dashboard rather than going back for them.
# Project settings → API.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is compiled into the browser bundle, so ONLY the publishable key
# (older projects call it "anon public") may ever carry that prefix.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://PROJECT.supabase.co"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""
#
# The service-role key bypasses every row-level policy there will ever be. It
# belongs in server code only, and must NEVER take a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""
